<h2>4. Key Differentiators & Judge Q&A Cheat Sheet</h2>
<table>
  <tr>
    <th style="width: 30%;">Judge Question</th>
    <th style="width: 70%;">Winning Answer to Deliver</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>"How is this different from standard satellite heatmaps like Google Earth Engine or NASA FIRMS?"</strong></td>
    <td>Standard heatmaps are purely descriptive—they show where it was hot in the past. UrbanIQ is <strong>prescriptive and predictive</strong>. It couples satellite telemetry with a Physics-Informed ML surrogate that predicts future surface temperature deltas when specific urban interventions (cool roofs, afforestation, reflective pavements) are applied, subject to financial budget constraints.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>"Why use Voronoi tessellation instead of arbitrary grid squares?"</strong></td>
    <td>Grid squares ignore municipal administrative reality. City budgets, zoning laws, and ward engineers operate by <strong>administrative ward boundaries</strong>. Our Voronoi tessellation generates contiguous, non-overlapping jurisdictional cells centered on actual Pune neighborhoods, enabling direct municipal accountability.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>"What machine learning model powers the simulation?"</strong></td>
    <td>We utilize a <strong>HistGradientBoostingRegressor / Physics-Informed surrogate</strong> trained on multi-spectral satellite features (NDVI, NDBI, spatial coordinates). It ensures physical conservation of energy: increasing albedo and canopy density strictly decreases surface temperature according to thermodynamic boundary layer equations.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>"Does the system work offline or in disaster scenarios?"</strong></td>
    <td>Yes! We implemented a robust dual-mode architecture. If the backend API is unreachable or deployed in low-connectivity disaster relief setups, the frontend automatically falls back to an embedded high-resolution Pune sample dataset with local client-side physics simulation.</td>
  </tr>
</table>

